   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to the tobacco use and\nprevention program; and to amend the tax law, in relation to the registration\nof vendors of electronic cigarettes",
    "basePrintNo": "S301",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S301-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1399-ii, Pub Health L; add Art 20-C §492, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S301\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to the tobacco use\nand prevention program; and to amend the tax law, in relation to the\nregistration of vendors of electronic cigarettes\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo curb electronic cigarette use among minors through prevention, educa-\ntion and sale to minor compliance checks.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the Tobacco Use Prevention and Control Program to\nexpand current school-based programs and marketing initiatives aimed at\nreducing tobacco use, to include electronic cigarette and liquid nico-\ntine use. This section also charges with Tobacco Use Prevention and\nControl Advisory Board to advise the Commissioner on prevention and\ncontrol of electronic cigarette and liquid nicotine use amongst minors.\n\nSection two adds a new Article 20-C to the Tax Law, requiring any person\nselling or offering for sale electronic cigarettes or liquid nicotine,\nwho that is not registered with the Department of Taxation and Finance\nto sell tobacco products, to register under this section.  The Commis-\nsioner shall adopt regulations to establish the registration requirement\nand ensure compliance.\n\nSection three provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2012, the state banned the sale of electronic cigarettes to minors\nunder the age of 18. Despite enactment of this ban to minors in New York\nand a number of other states, the CDC reported that use of electronic\ncigarettes among middle school and high school students more than\ndoubled from 2011 to 2012. Recognizing that the health effects of elec-\ntronic cigarettes remain unknown, the FDA issued regulations on elec-\ntronic cigarettes, which includes a nationwide ban of the sale to minors\nin 2016. However, given the still increasing use among minors, the FDA\nrecently announced the Youth Tobacco Prevention Plan to investigate and\ncrack down on access to minors.\n\nThis legislation seeks to educate and discourage use amongst minors and\naddresses a loophole in electronic cigarette enforcement. Chapter 448 of\n2012 included electronic cigarettes in the Public Health Law provisions\nbanning sale to minors. Chapter 542 of 2014 expressly included liquid\nnicotine in the ban to minors. Given these changes did not add electron-\nic cigarettes or liquid nicotine to the definition of a \"tobacco prod-\nuct\" under the Tax Law, retailer's that sell only these products and not\ntraditional tobacco products do not need a tobacco registration from the\nstate. As a result, when these retailers are not subject to compliance\nchecks by the Department of Health Enforcement Unit to ensure they are\nnot selling to minors. By requiring retailers that are not registered to\nsell tobacco products, to register under this new article will ensure\nthat State and localities can conduct compliance checks to confirm\nadherence to existing law.\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.8609-A of 2018 (Hannon): Passed Senate\nA.11211 of 2018 (Rosenthal):  Died in Health\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nEffective the first day of the month commencing after the 180th day\nafter enactment; provided the addition, amendment and/or repeal of any\nrule or regulation necessary for implementation is effective immediate-\nly.\n",
